#import "@local/fgbooks:0.14.0": speech, stanza

== THE SECOND STAGE

Then Christian got ready to go back; and Evangelist, after he had kissed him, gave him one smile and wished him God speed. So he went on in haste, and he spoke to no man on the way; and if anyone asked him anything, he would not give them an answer. He went like one who was treading on forbidden ground the whole time, and he could by no means feel safe until he had got back onto the way he had left to follow Mr. Worldly Wiseman’s advice. So in time Christian came up to the gate. Now over the gate was written, “Knock, and it will be opened to you.” Matt 7:7.

So he knocked, more than once or twice, saying,

#quote[“May I now enter here? Will he within \
Open to sorry me, though I have been \
An undeserving rebel? Then shall I \
Not fail to sing his lasting praise on high.”]

At last a grave person named Goodwill came to the gate, and asked who was there, where he came from, and what he wanted.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Here is a poor burdened sinner. I come from the city of Destruction, but I am going to Mount Zion, so that I may be delivered from the wrath to come. So, sir, since I am told that the way there lies through this gate, I would like to know whether you are willing to let me in.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: I am willing with all my heart, he said; and with that he opened the gate.]

So when Christian was stepping in, the other gave him a pull. Then Christian said, What does that mean? The other told him, A little way from this gate a strong castle has been built, and Beelzebub is its captain. From there both he and those who are with him shoot arrows at those who come up to this gate, in the hope that they may die before they can enter. Then Christian said, I rejoice and tremble. So when he had got in, the man of the Gate asked him who had directed him there.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Evangelist told me to come here and knock, as I did; and he said that you, sir, would tell me what I must do.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: An open door is set before you, and no man can shut it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Now I begin to reap the benefits of the risks I have run.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: But how is it that you have come alone?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Because none of my neighbors saw their danger as I saw mine.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: Did any of them know you were coming?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, my wife and children saw me at the start, and called after me to turn back; and some of my neighbors also stood crying and calling after me to come back; but I put my fingers in my ears, and so came on my way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: But did none of them follow you, to persuade you to go back?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, both Obstinate and Pliable; but when they saw that they could not succeed, Obstinate went back railing; but Pliable came with me a little way.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: But why did he not come all the way?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: We did indeed come together until we came to the Slough of Despond, into which we both suddenly fell. And then my neighbor Pliable lost heart, and would not risk going any farther. So, getting out again on the side nearest his own house, he told me I could have the fine country alone, for all he cared: so he went his way, and I came mine; he after Obstinate, and I to this gate.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: Then Goodwill said, Alas, poor man! Is the celestial glory worth so little to him that he does not think it worth running the risk of a few difficulties to gain it?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Truly, said Christian, I have told the truth about Pliable; and if I were also to tell all the truth about myself, it would be clear that I am no better than he is. It is true he went back to his own house, but I also turned aside to go in the way of death, persuaded to it by the carnal arguments of one Mr. Worldly Wiseman.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: Oh, did he come upon you? What! He wanted you to look for ease at the hands of Mr. Legality! They are both of them nothing but cheats. But did you take his advice?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, as far as I dared. I went to find Mr. Legality, until I thought that the mountain that stands by his house would fall on my head; so there I was forced to stop.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: That mountain has been the death of many, and will be the death of many more: it is good that you escaped being dashed to pieces by it.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, truly, I do not know what would have become of me there, if Evangelist had not fortunately met me again as I stood musing, down in the dumps; but it was God’s mercy that he came to me again, for otherwise I would never have come here. But now I have come, such as I am, more fit indeed to die by that mountain than to stand here talking with my Lord. But O, what a favor this is to me, that I am let in here after all!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: We make no objection to anyone, whatever they have done before they come here; they are by no means cast out. John 6:37. And so, good Christian, come a little way with me, and I will teach you about the way you must go. Look ahead of you; do you see this narrow way? That is the way you must go. It was laid out by the patriarchs, prophets, Christ, and his apostles, and it is as straight as a rule can make it; this is the way you must go.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But, said Christian, are there no turnings or windings by which a stranger may lose his way?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Goodwill]: Yes, there are many ways that run into this one, and they are crooked and wide; but this is how you can tell the right from the wrong: only the right one is straight and narrow. Matt 7:14.]

Then I saw in my dream that Christian asked him further whether he could help him off with the burden that was upon his back. For as yet he had not got rid of it, and he could not by any means get it off without help.

He told him, “As for your burden, be content to bear it until you come to the place of deliverance; for there it will fall from your back by itself.”

Then Christian began to gird up his loins and get ready for his journey. So the other told him that when he had gone some distance from the gate, he would come to the house of the Interpreter, and he should knock at his door, and the Interpreter would show him excellent things. Then Christian took leave of his friend, and Goodwill again wished him God speed.

Then he went on till he came to the house of the Interpreter,#footnote[The Holy Spirit.] where he knocked over and over. At last someone came to the door and asked who was there.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, here is a traveler, who was told by an acquaintance of the head of this house to call here for my benefit; so I would like to speak with the master of the house.]

So he called for the master of the house, who after a little while came to Christian and asked him what he wanted.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Sir, said Christian, I am a man who has come from the city of Destruction, and I am going to Mount Zion; and I was told by the man who stands at the gate at the head of this way that if I called here you would show me excellent things, such as would help me on my journey.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Then Interpreter said, Come in; I will show you what will do you good. So he told his servant to light the candle, and told Christian to follow him; so he took him into a private room, and told his servant to open a door. When the servant had done so, Christian saw the picture of a very grave person hanging on the wall; and this is what it looked like: It had its eyes lifted up to heaven, the best of books in its hand, the law of truth was written on its lips, the world was behind its back; it stood as if it were pleading with men, and a crown of gold hung over its head.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said, What does this mean?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: The man whose picture this is, is one in a thousand: he can father children, 1 Cor 4:15, labor in birth with children, Gal 4:19, and nurse them himself when they are born. And as you see him with his eyes lifted up to heaven, the best of books in his hand, and the law of truth written on his lips, it is to show you that his work is to know dark things and make them plain to sinners; just as you also see him standing as if he were pleading with men. And as you see the world cast behind him, and a crown hanging over his head, that is to show you that, as he slights and despises the things that are present for the love he has for his Master’s service, he is sure to have glory as his reward in the world that comes next. Now, said the Interpreter, I have shown you this picture first because the man whose picture this is, is the only man whom the Lord of the place where you are going has authorized to be your guide in all the difficult places you may meet on the way. So pay good attention to what I have shown you, and keep well in mind what you have seen, in case on your journey you meet some who pretend to lead you right, but their way goes down to death.]

Then he took him by the hand and led him into a very large parlor that was full of dust, because it had never been swept. After the Interpreter had looked it over for a little while, he called for a man to sweep. Now when the man began to sweep, the dust flew about so thickly that Christian was almost choked by it. Then the Interpreter said to a maid who stood by, “Bring water here, and sprinkle the room”; and when she had done so, it was swept and cleansed with pleasure.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said, What does this mean?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: The Interpreter answered, This parlor is the heart of a man who was never sanctified by the sweet grace of the Gospel. The dust is his original sin and inward corruptions, which have defiled the whole man. He who began to sweep at first is the law; but she who brought water and sprinkled it is the Gospel. Now you saw that as soon as the first began to sweep, the dust flew about so much that he could not clean the room, and you were almost choked by it. This is to show you that the law, instead of cleansing the heart from sin (by its working), revives sin, Rom 7:9, puts strength into it, 1 Cor 15:56, and increases it in the soul, Rom 5:20, even as it reveals and forbids it; for it does not give power to subdue it. Again, you saw the maid sprinkle the room with water, and then it was cleansed with pleasure. This is to show you that when the Gospel comes into the heart with its sweet and precious influences, then, I say, just as you saw the maid lay the dust by sprinkling the floor with water, so sin is conquered and subdued, and the soul made clean, through faith in it, and so made fit for the King of glory to dwell in. John 15:3; Eph 5:26; Acts 15:9; Rom 16:25, 26.]

I also saw in my dream that the Interpreter took him by the hand and led him into a little room, where two little children sat, each in his own chair. The name of the elder was Passion, and the name of the other Patience. Passion seemed very discontented, but Patience was very quiet. Then Christian asked, “Why is Passion so discontented?” The Interpreter answered, “Their guardian wants him to wait for his best things till the beginning of next year, but he must have everything now; Patience, though, is willing to wait.”

Then I saw that someone came to Passion, brought him a bag of treasure, and poured it out at his feet. He picked it up and rejoiced over it, and laughed at Patience in scorn as well. But I watched only a little while, and he had squandered it all, and had nothing left but rags.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said to the Interpreter, Explain this matter to me more fully.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: So he said, These two boys are pictures: Passion, of the men of this world, and Patience, of the men of the world to come. For, as you see here, passion must have everything now, this year, that is to say, in this world; and so it is with the men of this world. They must have all their good things now; they cannot wait till next year, that is, until the next world, for their share of good. That proverb, “A bird in the hand is worth two in the bush,” carries more weight with them than all the divine testimonies of the good of the world to come. But as you saw that he had quickly squandered everything, and soon had nothing left but rags, so it will be with all such men at the end of this world.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said, Now I see that Patience has the best wisdom, and that on many counts. 1. Because he waits for the best things. 2. And also because he will have the glory of his when the other has nothing but rags.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Yes, and you may add another, namely, the glory of the next world will never wear out, but these are suddenly gone. So Passion did not have as much reason to laugh at Patience because he had his good things first, as Patience will have to laugh at Passion because he had his best things last; for first must give way to last, because last must have its time to come; but last gives way to nothing, for there is no other to follow it. So he who has his portion first must have a time when he spends it; but he who has his portion last will have it for ever. That is why it is said of Dives, “During your lifetime you received your good things, while Lazarus received bad things. But now he is comforted, while you are in agony.” Luke 16:25.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then I see that it is not best to covet the things that are now, but to wait for things to come.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: You speak the truth: for the things that are seen are temporal, but the things that are not seen are eternal. 2 Cor 4:18. But though this is so, yet because things present and our fleshly appetite are such close neighbors to each other, and again, because things to come and our carnal sense are such strangers to each other, that is why the first of these so quickly become friends, and the second are kept so far apart.]

Then I saw in my dream that the Interpreter took Christian by the hand and led him into a place where a fire was burning against a wall, and someone was standing by it, always throwing a great deal of water on it to put it out; yet the fire burned higher and hotter.

Then Christian said, What does this mean?

The Interpreter answered, This fire is the work of grace that is done in the heart; the one who throws water on it, to extinguish it and put it out, is the devil. But as you see the fire burning higher and hotter in spite of it, you will also see the reason for that. So he took him around to the back of the wall, where he saw a man with a jar of oil in his hand, which he also kept pouring (but secretly) into the fire.

Then Christian said, What does this mean?

The Interpreter answered, This is Christ, who continually, with the oil of his grace, keeps up the work already begun in the heart; by which means, whatever the devil can do, the souls of his people remain full of grace. 2 Cor 12:9. And as you saw that the man stood behind the wall to keep the fire going, this is to teach you that it is hard for the tempted to see how this work of grace is kept up in the soul.

I also saw that the Interpreter took him by the hand again and led him into a pleasant place, where a stately palace had been built, beautiful to see; and Christian was greatly delighted at the sight of it. He also saw certain people walking on top of it, who were clothed all in gold.

Then Christian said, May we go in there?

Then the Interpreter took him and led him up toward the door of the palace; and there at the door stood a great company of men, wanting to go in, but they did not dare. A man also sat at a little distance from the door, at a table, with a book and his inkhorn in front of him, to take down the names of those who were to enter; and Christian saw that many men in armor stood in the doorway to guard it, resolved to do the men who wanted to enter whatever hurt and harm they could. Now Christian was somewhat bewildered. At last, when every man was shrinking back for fear of the armed men, Christian saw a man with a very resolute face come up to the man who sat there to write, saying, “Set down my name, sir”; and when the writer had done so, Christian saw the man draw his sword, put a helmet on his head, and rush toward the door at the armed men, who fell on him with deadly force; but the man, not at all discouraged, fell to cutting and hacking most fiercely. So after he had received and given many wounds to those who tried to keep him out, Matt 11:12; Acts 14:22; he cut his way through them all and pressed forward into the palace. At this a pleasant voice was heard from those who were inside, even from those who walked on top of the palace, saying,

#quote[“Come in, come in, \
Eternal glory thou shalt win.”]

So he went in, and was clothed with garments like theirs. Then Christian smiled and said, I really think I know the meaning of this.

Now, said Christian, let me go on from here. No, stay, said the Interpreter, till I have shown you a little more, and after that you shall go on your way. So he took him by the hand again and led him into a very dark room, where a man sat in an iron cage.

Now the man looked very sad; he sat with his eyes cast down to the ground, his hands folded together, and he sighed as if his heart would break. Then Christian said, What does this mean? At this the Interpreter told him to talk with the man.

Then Christian said to the man, What are you? The man answered, I am what I once was not.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: What were you once?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: The man said, I was once a fair and flourishing professing Christian, Luke 8:13, both in my own eyes and in the eyes of others. I was once, as I thought, well on the way to the celestial city, and I even had joy then at the thought that I would get there.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, but what are you now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: I am now a man of despair, and am shut up in it, as in this iron cage. I cannot get out. Oh, now I cannot!]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But how did you come into this condition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: I stopped watching and being sober; I gave my lusts free rein; I sinned against the light of the word and the goodness of God; I have grieved the Spirit, and he is gone; I tempted the devil, and he has come to me; I have provoked God to anger, and he has left me; I have so hardened my heart that I cannot repent.]

Then Christian said to the Interpreter, But is there no hope for such a man as this? Ask him, said the Interpreter.

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Then Christian said, Is there no hope? Must you be kept in the iron cage of despair?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: No, none at all.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Why, the Son of the Blessed is full of compassion.]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: I have crucified him all over again to myself, Heb 6:6; I have despised his person, Luke 19:14; I have despised his righteousness; I have counted his blood an unholy thing; I have insulted the spirit of grace, Heb 10:29. So I have shut myself out of all the promises, and now nothing remains for me but threats, dreadful threats, faithful threats of certain judgment and fiery indignation, which will devour me as an adversary.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: For what did you bring yourself into this condition?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: For the lusts, pleasures, and profits of this world, in the enjoyment of which I then promised myself much delight; but now every one of those things also bites me and gnaws me like a burning worm.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But can you not repent and turn now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: God has denied me repentance. His word gives me no encouragement to believe; indeed, he himself has shut me up in this iron cage, and all the men in the world cannot let me out. Oh, eternity! eternity! How will I grapple with the misery that I must meet in eternity?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Then the Interpreter said to Christian, Remember this man’s misery, and let it be an everlasting warning to you.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Well, said Christian, this is fearful! God help me to watch and be sober, and to pray that I may avoid the cause of this man’s misery. Sir, is it not time for me to go on my way now?]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Wait till I show you one thing more, and then you shall go on your way.]

So he took Christian by the hand again and led him into a bedroom where someone was getting out of bed; and as he put on his clothes, he shook and trembled. Then Christian said, Why does this man tremble so? The Interpreter then told the man to tell Christian why he was doing so.

So he began, and said, “Last night, as I was sleeping, I dreamed, and behold, the heavens grew extremely black; it also thundered and lightning flashed in the most fearful way, so that it put me into an agony. So I looked up in my dream, and saw the clouds racing along at an unusual rate; at which I heard a great sound of a trumpet, and also saw a man sitting on a cloud, attended by the thousands of heaven: they were all in flaming fire, and the heavens too were in a burning flame. Then I heard a voice saying, ‘Arise, you#super[+] dead, and come to judgment.’ And with that the rocks split, the graves opened, and the dead that were in them came out. Some of them were exceedingly glad, and looked upward; and some tried to hide themselves under the mountains. Then I saw the man who sat on the cloud open the book, and call the world to come near. Yet, because of a fierce flame that issued out and came from before him, there was a fitting distance between him and them, as between the judge and the prisoners at the bar. 1 Cor 15; 1 Thess 4:16; Jude 15; John 5:28, 29; 2 Thess 1:8–10; Rev 20:11–14; Isa 26:21; Mic 7:16, 17; Ps 5:4; 50:1–3; Mal 3:2, 3; Dan 7:9, 10. I also heard it proclaimed to those who attended the man who sat on the cloud, ‘Gather together the tares, the chaff, and stubble, and cast them into the burning lake.’ Matt 3:12; 13:30; 24:30; Mal 4:1. And with that the bottomless pit opened, just about where I stood; and out of its mouth came great quantities of smoke and coals of fire, with hideous noises. It was also said to the same persons, ‘Gather my wheat into the barn.’ Luke 3:17. And with that I saw many caught up and carried away into the clouds, but I was left behind. 1 Thess 4:16, 17. I also tried to hide myself, but I could not, for the man who sat on the cloud kept his eye on me all the time; my sins also came into my mind, and my conscience accused me on every side. Rom 2:14, 15. At this I woke from my sleep.”

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: But what was it that made you so afraid of this sight?]

#speech[#smallcaps[The Man]: Why, I thought that the day of judgment had come, and that I was not ready for it; but what frightened me most was that the angels gathered up several, and left me behind. Also the pit of hell opened its mouth just where I stood. My conscience too afflicted me; and, as I thought, the Judge always had his eye on me, showing indignation in his face.]

Then the Interpreter said to Christian, “Have you considered all these things?”

#speech[#smallcaps[Christian]: Yes, and they make me both hope and fear.]

#speech[#smallcaps[Interpreter]: Well, keep all these things in your mind in such a way that they may be like a goad in your sides, to drive you forward in the way you must go. Then Christian began to gird up his loins and get ready for his journey. Then the Interpreter said, “May the Comforter be always with you, good Christian, to guide you in the way that leads to the city.” So Christian went on his way, saying,]

#quote[“Here I have seen things rare and profitable, \
Things pleasant, dreadful, things to make me stable \
In what I have begun to take in hand: \
Then let me think on them, and understand \
Wherefore they showed me were, and let me be \
Thankful, O good Interpreter, to thee.”]
